1. **An apparatus for managing access by a plurality of devices to federated areas, the apparatus comprising:**

   one or more processors; and

   one or more non-transitory computer-readable storage media storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the apparatus to:

   define a shared base federated area accessible by a first device and a second device, the shared base federated area including a plurality of base objects and one or more base task routines associated with the plurality of base objects;

   define a first device-specific federated area associated with the first device and a second device-specific federated area associated with the second device, each of the first device-specific federated area and the second device-specific federated area being logically distinct from the shared base federated area and from each other;

   provide, to the first device-specific federated area and the second device-specific federated area, inherited access to at least a portion of the plurality of base objects from the shared base federated area, such that an inherited base object is usable within a corresponding device-specific federated area without duplicating the inherited base object in the corresponding device-specific federated area;

   store, in the first device-specific federated area, one or more first device-specific objects or first device-specific task routines available to the first device;

   store, in the second device-specific federated area, one or more second device-specific objects or second device-specific task routines available to the second device;

   receive, from the first device or the second device, a request to access an object or execute a task routine;

   determine, based on an identity of the requesting device, a corresponding device-specific federated area for the requesting device;

   resolve the request by searching the corresponding device-specific federated area and the shared base federated area, wherein objects from the shared base federated area are inherited into the corresponding device-specific federated area; and

   in response to determining that a task routine in the corresponding device-specific federated area and a task routine in the shared base federated area are both applicable to the request, execute or select the task routine in the corresponding device-specific federated area in priority over the task routine in the shared base federated area.

1. A computing apparatus comprising:  

   one or more processors; and  

   one or more non-transitory computer-readable storage media storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the computing apparatus to:  

   maintain a base federated area associated with a plurality of devices and a plurality of objects accessible to the plurality of devices;  

   maintain a first federated area and a second federated area, each of the first federated area and the second federated area being associated with a respective subset of the plurality of devices;  

   assign, for each of the first federated area and the second federated area, one or more area-specific access parameters defining device access to one or more objects;  

   determine, for a device associated with at least one of the first federated area or the second federated area, an inherited object accessibility based at least in part on object accessibility defined for the base federated area and object accessibility defined for the at least one of the first federated area or the second federated area;  

   control access by the device to an object according to the inherited object accessibility;  

   maintain a plurality of task routines executable in association with the base federated area, the first federated area, or the second federated area;  

   assign priority rules to the plurality of task routines, the priority rules defining a relative execution priority among task routines associated with different ones of the base federated area, the first federated area, and the second federated area; and  

   in response to detecting a condition for execution of two or more task routines having overlapping control authority with respect to the device or the object, select or order execution of the two or more task routines according to the priority rules.

A processor configured to:

1. maintain a plurality of federated storage areas, each federated storage area being associated with one or more access-control attributes;

2. store, for each of a plurality of devices, device-specific access rights identifying at least one federated storage area accessible by the respective device;

3. maintain an inheritance relationship among at least a subset of the plurality of federated storage areas, wherein a child federated storage area inherits at least one access-control attribute from a parent federated storage area unless overridden by an access-control attribute assigned to the child federated storage area;

4. maintain a priority relationship among at least a subset of the plurality of federated storage areas, wherein the priority relationship defines an order for resolving conflicting access-control attributes associated with two or more federated storage areas;

5. receive, from a requesting device, a request to access data associated with a selected federated storage area;

6. determine, based at least in part on the device-specific access rights, the inheritance relationship, and the priority relationship, whether the requesting device is authorized to access the selected federated storage area;

7. resolve any conflict between inherited access-control attributes and directly assigned access-control attributes according to the priority relationship; and

8. grant or deny the requesting device access to the selected federated storage area based on the determination,

wherein different devices are granted access to different federated storage areas, and wherein access to each federated storage area is controlled while preserving the inheritance and priority relationships among the plurality of federated storage areas.
